{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 / Mathlib proof of Theorem 2 of Chetwynd and Hilton, \"1-factorizing regular graphs of high degree — an improved bound\" (Discrete Math. 75 (1989)): a d-regular simple graph of even order with d ≥ ½(√7 − 1)|V| (≈ 0.823|V|) is d-edge-colourable (CH89.lineGraph_colorable_of_isRegular_sqrt7), with the integer form |V| = 2n, 7n² ≤ (d + n)² (CH89.lineGraph_colorable_of_isRegular_nat). The article's proof of Theorem 1 contains an unjustified step (\"we may moreover suppose that x_j comes before w_j in the list (r_m, …, r_t, l_1, …, l_m)\"), which fails for an arbitrary maximal matching M₀; the formalization repairs it by choosing M₀ to minimize −K·#covered + max(T, T′), T = u_Y − u_X − μ, ordering the edges of M₀ (mixed ones outside, XX/YY alternately) and pairing X with Y by Hall's theorem with one dummy, so that at most one pair is bad, which the article's extra step q + 1 absorbs. The constant ½(√7 − 1) of the article is obtained exactly. Theorem 1 (staircase classes peeled off as near 1-factors, core of Theorem 5, re-insertion of v* and w) is assembled from the pinned dependency PALOMAR-2026-11-04-000016; Lemma 3 (a pair with r(r − 1) ≤ (2n − 1)p common non-neighbours) and the parameter inequality r(r − 1) − (2n − 1)(5n − 3d − 1) = (d + n)² − 7n² + n + 1 are proved here. Two statements; no definitions beyond Mathlib's.",
